I love Halloween almost as much as I  love wearing tights it's the only day of  the year when everyone's allowed to  dress up the way they normally would if  society didn't have so many hang-ups  about people and hockey masks with blood  all over them it's the day when you can  leave all the entrails and cobwebs in  your house and just tell everyone  they're decorations but on this recent  Halloween in particular I felt like  testing an age-old bit of controversy in  the comics that has never had a  definitive answer  how did spider-man make such a kick-ass  costume when he's poor and has no sewing  experience most teenage boys I know  aren't that great at sewing so he must  have had a difficult time putting it  together the adaptations ever since have  always kind of glossed over it one way  or another the Sam Raimi movies show him  drawing it and then he just has it one  day and it looks like $100,000 rubber  suit with no visible seams then Andrew  Garfield made his buy of something to do  with silk-screening and sunglasses  they'll really cover the gritty details  either although I do like how his suit  looks kind of unfinished messy then he  too just wakes up one day and here's the  perfect one Tom Hollands spider-man just  had a billionaire throw it together in  an afternoon and he didn't do any of the  work on it even in the Ultimate Comics  it's a professional suit made by a  Wrestling Federation and he just draw  some lines on it I guess what we can  gather from these examples is that him  making himself is kind of unrealistic  and they either just say he did all the  hard part's off-screen or he had someone  else do it well let's test that with  less than $100 and some junk I found at  a Halloween store a few weeks back I'll  build my own suit I have about as much  sewing skill just as limited resources  as Peter Parker and we're about the same  age so why not give it a shot I think  using the comics as a sort of blueprint  for all the pieces we could build  something pretty cool the first step is  gathering materials the spider-man  ultimate guide has an entry about Peter  hearing that his schools dance team was  throwing away a bunch of spandex body  suits  so naturally he does his dumpster diving  routine and finds one that fits and  grabs a couple of extra ones for  additional material to cut up that means  he didn't have to take all the annoying  measurements for every square inch of  his body and then cut a log of fabric to  fit a pattern  he had a suit though is his size and he  just attached a bunch of extra parts to  it like any lazy ass teenager would  perfect I'll find something similar  during October I worked at a local  Halloween store that's only open  seasonally and I found a bunch of lycra  bodysuits near the back of the store  that were just not getting sold so I  bought them myself with the intention of  slicing them up with scissors close  enough and I also found these cat  goggles in there with reflective plastic  lenses sure these were probably meant  for like a Catwoman costume but they'll  suit my needs I figure Aunt May has a  sewing machine and various equipment  lying around the house since she is an  old lady and we all know every woman  over the age of 50 owns a vast amount of  sewing supplies now the fun part  attaching plastic disband X without  ruining either of them well I could just  wear the goggles over the mask like a  chump but we can do better than that  that means I'll grab a dremel tool and  hack this thing in a shape the mask will  both be lighter and fit better with  these pieces segmented and attached on  their own I practice this with a pair of  spider-man sunglasses in a store-bought  costume for comic-con or later this year  I have to be really careful not to make  a stray mark that scratches the lenses  for safety I put cloth over the shiny  part it's also way easier to see through  this than white spandex I think it's a  little dumb how they always half-ass  drawing it just make the eyes solid  white and then the movies try to make it  solid white too if they're polarized  lenses how the hell does he see with  them at night whatever anyway after you  snap the glasses into a million pieces  and cover yourself in black dust you  drill a series of small holes in it to  thread a needle through  after that apply the secret to any good  superhero costume glue fabric glue to  keep the edges from fraying and liquid  stitch to attach the plastic then you  put the stitches in after the glue  hardens that way you have a backup if  the glue gives out and you have a backup  of the stitches snap which is liable to  happen in this line of work man this is  a pain in the ass make sure you look up  the basics of stitching before you do  this not super complicated or hard to  learn lots of different kinds of not see  out to tie to make it look neat after  you jab yourself in the finger with a  needle a dozen times you have yourself a  mask following that step I put on the  red suit and made the marks for where  the lines will be when I cut it which  basically meant I just sat there drawing  on myself with a sharpie that bled  through to my skin anyway  I imagine he probably did the smarter  thing and took the spandex bodysuit and  folded it in half so his lines were  symmetrical but he also didn't have to  make a video tutorial well now comes the  scary part can't exactly get a do-over  on this just duh just be careful okay  make sure the lines are drawn a little  bigger than what you'll actually use  because you need to fold it over and  smooth out the edges this is a process  called hemming it's so the stupid thing  doesn't fall apart and unravel in about  two seconds from all the strings hanging  out of it make sure to buy a lot of this  stuff because you should put it on every  single edge to be safe make sure you cut  around the zipper on the red suit  because the blue suit is the one where  the zipper will remain mostly intact  after I got the red piece all cut out  and ready I needed to tend to the blue  bodysuit a bit basically I have to slice  off the hands feet and mask and then hem  up the line to make sure the zipper is  still usable after slicing it in half  with absurdly sharp scissors keep the  thingy below the neck of the suit and  then create a makeshift stopper for it  by stitching around the zipper a few  dozen times  it's janky but it works next getting  precise measurements and putting the  pins into it isn't exactly something I  can give you advice for it's just hard  to do when you'll wish you had an extra  set of hands to do it  stringing up a sewing machine is  something you can either learn through  trial and error or just ask really vague  leading questions to Aunt May just tell  her you want to make your own sweater  vests from now on and you need to learn  these kinds of things as you grow up  hemming lines is super easy if you're  competent know what you're doing as you  can tell by how straight that stitch is  I'm not competent or know what I'm doing  the worst thing about a secret identity  is you can't ask someone else to do your  sewing now after snipping the blue suit  down to fit what we need you can start  erratically doing whatever you feel like  in no particular pattern to put off  using the sewing machine and hurting  your pride anymore  so let's draw on some lines first step  is to go into the appropriately themed  lavatory and put the mask on so it's  stretched out properly on your head then  instead of silk-screening it just get  lazy and draw the lines on with a marker  what could go wrong after washing the  lines off of your face and pushing out  of that like a huge coward find a  mannequin to put the mask on as soon as  you come to the realization that you  don't have a mannequin it's important to  follow these steps to the letter 1 find  an old unrelated Halloween mask in your  closet to stuff it full of hand towels  until it resembles the size and shape of  the 